===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|abelmusk|abelmosch||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|NL.|Abelmoschus|abelmoschus}}, from {{der|en|ar|حَبّ الْمِسْك|t=pills of musk}}. {{root|en|ar|م س ك|ح ب ب}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈeɪbəlmɒsk/|/ˈeɪblmɒsk/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈeɪbəlˌmɑsk/|/ˈeɪblmɑsk/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abelmosk.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪbəlmɒsk|eɪblmɒsk|eɪbəlmɑsk|eɪblmɑsk|s1=3|s2=2|s3=3|s4=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[edible]] and [[aromatic]] [[seed pod]]s ([[properly]], [[capsule]]s) of the {{taxfmt|Abelmoschus moschatus|species}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1719|author=Joseph Pitton de Tournefort|title=The Compleat Herbal|volume=I|page=70
|passage=Egyptian Ketmia, with a perfumed or Musk-Seed..., called '''Abelmosch''' of Morison.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1892|translator=Carl Deite|author=William Theodore Brannt|title=A Practical Treatise on the Manufacture of Perfumery|page=230
|passage='''Abelmosk''' grains are the seeds of a plant... indigenous to Central Africa, Arabia, and India.}}
# The [[tropical]] [[evergreen]] [[shrub]] {{taxfmt|Abelmoschus moschatus|species}} [[itself]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1846|author=W. M. Buchanan|title=A Technological Dictionary|page=4
|passage='''''Abelmosk''''', ''Abelmosch'', or ''Abelmusk'', the Syrian mallow, or musk okro, a species of hibiscus (H. abelmoschus).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Richard A. Spears|title=Language & Civilization|volume=I|page=43
|passage=The same description fits '''abelmosk''', the ''Hibiscus abelmoshus'', better known to the world as the East Indian dwarf okra plant.}}
# {{lb|en|uncommon|usually|_|in the plural}} [[other|Other]] [[member]]s of the [[genus]] {{taxfmt|Abelmoschus|genus}}, [[such as]] [[okra]].

====Usage notes====
Formerly considered a species of [[hibiscus]], but since distinguished as part of a separate genus.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|A. moschatus}} {{vern|annual hibiscus}}, {{vern|tropical jewel hibiscus}} {{q|dated}}; {{l|en|ambrette}}, {{vern|muskdana}}, {{l|en|musk mallow}}, {{vern|musk okra}}, {{vern|ornamental okra}}, {{l|en|rose mallow}}, {{vern|Yorka okra}}, {{l|en|musk seed}}

